    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/04C041120ED4B4CA39C09ACAC69625CF/jobs/339840": {
      "title": "Grants Manager (Full-time, Onsite)",
      "description": "Grants Manager (Full-time, Onsite) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Grants Manager (Full-time, Onsite) Wilson College - Chambersburg, PA 17201 Apply Overview Position Type Full Time Education Level 4 Year Degree Apply Description Wilson College is seeking a collaborative and detail-oriented Grants Manager to support the College’s comprehensive grants program and advance institutional priorities through government, corporate, and private foundation funding. The Grants Manager plays an important role in identifying funding opportunities, developing competitive proposals, coordinating grant activities, and supporting effective reporting and stewardship. This position works closely with faculty, staff, and College leadership to research and develop fundable projects, prepare grant proposals and reports, and maintain relationships with current and prospective funding partners. The successful candidate will be an organized and persuasive writer who can work both independently and collaboratively while managing multiple projects, priorities, and deadlines. Essential Responsibilities The Grants Manager will: Research foundation, corporate, state, and federal funding opportunities aligned with College priorities and faculty and staff funding needs. Develop and maintain a pipeline of prospective funding opportunities for the College. Assist faculty and staff in developing grant concepts and projects through interviews, research, information gathering, and assessment of funder guidelines. Prepare, write, edit, and submit grant proposals, applications, letters of inquiry, and other funding requests. Coordinate proposal development with faculty, staff, project managers, principal investigators, the Business Office, and other appropriate College departments. Review funding guidelines and requirements to ensure proposals and supporting materials are complete, accurate, and submitted by established deadlines. Coordinate awarded grants in collaboration with project managers/principal investigators, the Business Office, and other appropriate College departments. Assist with the preparation and submission of required grant reports, including gathering information from project managers, principal investigators, and other stakeholders. Maintain grant files, calendars, timelines, deadlines, and other documentation necessary for effective grant administration and stewardship. Maintain accurate records of grant proposals, awards, reports, funder interactions, visits, and stewardship activities in Raiser’s Edge and other College systems. Maintain and steward relationships with foundation, corporate, and government funding partners. Coordinate appropriate funder stewardship activities, including reports, correspondence, meetings, calls, and site visits. Assess, develop, maintain, and communicate effective grant-related policies, procedures, and best practices in collaboration with appropriate College departments. Represent Wilson College professionally in interactions with current and prospective funders and external partners. Perform other duties as assigned. Competencies Grant Research & Development : Ability to identify and evaluate government, corporate, and foundation funding opportunities and assess alignment with institutional priorities and funding needs. Grant Writing & Communication : Strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to develop clear, compelling, well-organized, and persuasive proposals and reports. Attention to Detail : Ability to interpret and follow complex proposal, reporting, compliance, and funder requirements while ensuring deadlines, documentation requirements, and donor intent are appropriately addressed. Project & Deadline Management : Ability to independently manage multiple proposals, reports, projects, timelines, and competing priorities while maintaining accuracy and organization. Relationship Building & Collaboration : Demonstrated ability to establish productive working relationships with faculty, staff, College leadership, project managers, principal investigators, funders, and external partners. Grant Administration & Stewardship : Ability to coordinate grant activities throughout the funding lifecycle, including proposal development, award administration, reporting, recordkeeping, and funder stewardship. Research & Analytical Skills : Ability to gather, evaluate, organize, and synthesize institutional, programmatic, financial, and external information for use in grant development and reporting. Confidentiality & Professional Judgment : Ability to exercise discretion and sound judgment when working with sensitive institutional, donor, financial, and personnel information. Technology & Data Management : Ability to effectively utilize fundraising, grants management, database, and other technology necessary to maintain accurate records and support grant activities. Adaptability & Initiative : Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively in a dynamic higher education environment while responding to changing institutional and funding priorities. Position Type & Expected Hours of Work Full-time, 12-month, exempt position. Regular work schedule is generally Monday through Friday during established College business hours. Some evening and weekend hours may be required to support funder meetings, College events, grant deadlines, and other related activities. The position may require occasional schedule flexibility based on grant deadlines, funder requirements, and institutional priorities. Education & Experience Bachelor’s degree preferred. One to three years of related experience in grant writing, fundraising, development, sponsored programs, higher education, or a related field preferred. Demonstrated experience with research, grant writing, proposal development, fundraising, or related activities preferred. Experience researching and working with government, corporate, and/or private foundation funding opportunities preferred. Experience preparing grant proposals, applications, reports, or other professional written materials preferred. Experience working collaboratively with faculty, staff, project leaders, or other internal stakeholders to develop proposals or projects preferred. Experience with Raiser’s Edge or a similar fundraising, grants management, or constituent relationship management system preferred. An equivalent combination of education and relevant experience may be considered. Working at Wilson College At Wilson College, we value our employees and are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to support your health, well-being, and professional growth. Full-time, on-site employees enjoy: Robust Health Benefits : Including medical, dental, and vision coverage, plus college-paid basic life and long-term disability insurance. Spending Account Options : Access to employee-funded Health Savings Accounts (HSAs) and Flexible Spending Accounts (FSAs) for both healthcare and dependent care needs. Retirement Planning : A Defined Contribution Retirement Plan with TIAA to help you plan for the future. Educational Benefits : Tuition remission for employees and their families for undergraduate, graduate, and certificate programs, as well as access to Tuition Exchange scholarships through a national consortium of colleges. Wilson fosters a supportive, collegial work environment that prioritizes work-life balance. Employees benefit from: Flexible dining options and free access to our on-campus fitness center. An Employee Assistance Program (EAP) offering confidential support and resources. As a qualified 501(c)(3) nonprofit employer, Wilson College employees may also be eligible for the Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) program. Share job details to About WILSON COLLEGE Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/04C041120ED4B4CA39C09ACAC69625CF/jobs/335968": {
      "title": "Career Counselor (Full-time, Onsite)",
      "description": "Career Counselor (Full-time, Onsite) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Career Counselor (Full-time, Onsite) Wilson College - Chambersburg, PA 17201 Apply Overview Position Type Full Time Education Level 4 Year Degree Apply Description Wilson College is seeking a student-centered and collaborative Career Counselor to join our Student Development team. The Career Counselor plays an important role in helping students and alumni explore career pathways, develop professional skills, pursue employment and internship opportunities, and prepare for graduate and professional education. This position provides individualized career coaching while developing and delivering engaging career programming designed to meet the evolving needs of Wilson students and the employment marketplace. The successful candidate will be an approachable and resourceful professional who enjoys building relationships with students, faculty, staff, alumni, employers, and community partners. Essential Responsibilities The Career Counselor will: Provide career advising and coaching to undergraduate, graduate, and adult students and alumni through individual appointments, drop-in services, virtual resources, workshops, and special events. Develop, deliver, and assess career development workshops and programs responsive to student needs and current employment trends. Assist students with resumes, cover letters, interviewing skills, professional presentation, and other career-readiness skills. Coach students on effective job and internship search strategies. Support students exploring and applying to graduate and professional programs. Help students identify career interests, skills, strengths, and potential academic and career pathways. Provide intentional outreach and support to students experiencing academic challenges, including helping students assess skills and explore alternative majors or career options. Utilize and maintain Handshake and other career-development resources for students and employers. Collaborate with the Alumni Office to develop career connections, networking opportunities, and other resources for current students. Connect students with appropriate campus, community, and external resources when additional support may be beneficial. Participate in New Student Orientation, Phoenix Leaders, and other student engagement initiatives. Develop social media and campus portal content designed to increase awareness of career services and student participation. Build productive relationships with employers, faculty, staff, alumni, and community partners. Serve on institutional committees and participate in the Student Development administrator-on-call rotation as assigned. Perform other duties as assigned. Competencies Student-Centered Service : Demonstrates a commitment to supporting students from diverse backgrounds, experiences, and stages of career development with professionalism, empathy, and responsiveness. Career Advising & Coaching : Ability to guide students and alumni in career exploration, employment preparation, internship and job searches, interviewing, and graduate or professional school planning Communication : Strong written, verbal, presentation, and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to communicate effectively with students, faculty, staff, alumni, employers, and community partners. Program Development & Facilitation : Ability to develop, deliver, and assess engaging workshops, programs, events, and resources that respond to student needs and evolving workforce trends. Relationship Building & Collaboration : Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships across the College and with employers, alumni, and community partners. Career & Workforce Knowledge : Knowledge of career development practices, employer recruiting strategies, job-market trends, occupational resources, and graduate and professional school pathways. Technology & Digital Engagement : Ability to effectively utilize career-services technology, including Handshake or similar platforms, as well as digital and social media tools to engage students and promote career resources. Organization & Initiative : Ability to independently manage multiple priorities, programs, appointments, and projects while exercising sound judgment and attention to detail. Adaptability & Problem-Solving : Ability to assess individual student needs, identify appropriate resources and solutions, and adapt services and programming to changing student and institutional needs. Teamwork : Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively within Student Development and across the College. Position Type & Expected Hours of Work Full-time, 12-month position. Regular work schedule is generally Monday through Friday during established College business hours. Some evening and weekend hours are required to support programs, events, and other departmental or College activities. Participation in the Student Development administrator-on-call rotation is required. The position may require occasional schedule flexibility based on student needs, programming, and institutional priorities. Education & Experience Bachelor’s degree required; master’s degree in higher education, student affairs, career development, human resources, or a related field preferred. Demonstrated experience advising, counseling, coaching, or supporting students or clients in career, employment, or professional development matters. Experience in higher education, career services, student affairs, workforce development, recruiting, or a related environment preferred. Experience developing and facilitating workshops, presentations, programs, or other educational initiatives preferred. Experience with Handshake or a similar career-services or recruiting platform preferred. An equivalent combination of education and relevant experience may be considered. Working at Wilson College At Wilson College, we value our employees and are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to support your health, well-being, and professional growth. Full-time, on-site employees enjoy: Robust Health Benefits : Including medical, dental, and vision coverage, plus college-paid basic life and long-term disability insurance. Spending Account Options : Access to employee-funded Health Savings Accounts (HSAs) and Flexible Spending Accounts (FSAs) for both healthcare and dependent care needs. Retirement Planning : A Defined Contribution Retirement Plan with TIAA to help you plan for the future. Educational Benefits : Tuition remission for employees and their families for undergraduate, graduate, and certificate programs, as well as access to Tuition Exchange scholarships through a national consortium of colleges. Wilson fosters a supportive, collegial work environment that prioritizes work-life balance. Employees benefit from: Flexible dining options and free access to our on-campus fitness center. An Employee Assistance Program (EAP) offering confidential support and resources. As a qualified 501(c)(3) nonprofit employer, Wilson College employees may also be eligible for the Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) program. Share job details to About WILSON COLLEGE Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
